# 4. Crypto libraries + tree-sitter

**All four noble packages released 2.x.4.0 on 2026-08-27, same day. All are hardening; I found no CVE and no breaking change in the .4.0 line.**

- **@noble/curves 2.4.0** — ECDH and ECDSA now **reject the identity point** even for codecs that permit it; DER parsing bounds signature/INTEGER sizes before bigint conversion (parser-DoS); FROST DKG hardened against round-one transcript substitution; POPRF inversion made constant-time; all security-sensitive arguments snapshotted against mutation. Credits 1Password Off-by-1 Labs. https://github.com/paulmillr/noble-curves/releases/tag/2.4.0
- **The real security event was 2.3.0 (2026-08-06), not 2.4.0** — a **remote timing attack on X25519** leaking up to **4.036 bits** of long-term private key across many samples (fingerprinting, not key recovery), reported by Stergiopoulos (AUEB) and Patsakis (Piraeus). Fixed with secret-scalar CSPRNG blinding and constant-time fixed-window multiply. Also Trail of Bits fixes: recovered ECDSA sigs now bound to their recovery id, non-canonical BLS encodings rejected, Edwards↔Montgomery helpers corrected. **If you are on 2.2.0, this is the upgrade that matters.** https://github.com/paulmillr/noble-curves/releases/tag/2.3.0
- **Perf note (2.3.0):** `getPublicKey`/`sign` got *slower* (window W=8→W=6 plus CT hardening). Restore with one line: `secp256k1.Point.BASE.precompute(8)`. Verification is up to +32% faster, BLS 2×. Same URL.
- **@noble/hashes 2.4.0** — options protected against mutation/pollution; **blake3 tree-merging fix for multi-terabyte streams**; webcrypto rejects engine-crashing output sizes; keccakprg fails until entropy added. **Behavioral change to watch: Argon2 cost options are now optional with defaults t:3, m:1 GiB, p:1, dkLen:32 and a 1 GiB maxmem cap — larger-memory calls must now set `maxmem` explicitly.** `nextTick`/`asyncLoop` yield via `scheduler.yield()` when available. https://github.com/paulmillr/noble-hashes/releases/tag/2.4.0
- **@noble/ciphers 2.4.0** — ChaCha/Salsa reject output buffers partially overlapping unread input; PRG throws after `clean()`; webcrypto snapshots keys and params. **2.3.0 was the behavior-changing one**: passing AAD to a cipher that doesn't support it now **throws instead of silently ignoring**, and CBC/ECB padding failures surface a generic `aes: bad decrypt` to cut padding-oracle signal. https://github.com/paulmillr/noble-ciphers/releases/tag/2.4.0
- **@noble/ed25519 3.2.0** — inputs snapshotted in `sign`/`signAsync`; `verify` rejects null options; actionable error when WebCrypto randomness is unavailable; `etc.invert()` now rejects modulus one as well as zero; ~10% code-size cut. https://github.com/paulmillr/noble-ed25519/releases/tag/3.2.0
- Note: **@noble/ed25519 3.0.1 (2026-03-17)** fixed a low-severity `verify` flaw where an attacker *with the secret key* could forge a signature valid for all messages under that key — impact limited to non-repudiation. https://github.com/paulmillr/noble-ed25519/releases/tag/3.0.1

**tree-sitter 0.27.0 (2026-08-30) — NOT breaking for a grammar built on 0.26.**

- **The ABI did not move.** `TREE_SITTER_LANGUAGE_VERSION` is **15** in both v0.26.11 and v0.27.0, and `TREE_SITTER_MIN_COMPATIBLE_LANGUAGE_VERSION` is **13** in both. A 0.26-generated parser loads unchanged. https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter/blob/v0.27.0/lib/include/tree_sitter/api.h
- **The one real hazard is the .wasm, not the ABI.** New docs added in this release: *"Some prebuilt `.wasm` files use an older dynamic-linking format that newer versions of `web-tree-sitter` cannot load, **even if their parser ABI is supported**. Rebuild these files using a current tree-sitter CLI."* If you ship a prebuilt grammar `.wasm`, **regenerate it with the 0.27 CLI**. https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter/pull/5884/files
- Grammar-DSL addition: **`eof()`** (`feat!`, PR #2488) — matches end-of-file without the `'\0'` hack that also matched embedded null bytes. Additive; existing grammars are unaffected. https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter/pull/2488
- Other `!`-marked change is API-surface only: `generate_parser_in_directory` now errors on a nonexistent *file* path (#5868). CLI gains concurrent builds of the same grammar at different paths (#5174) and `test --update` rewriting all corpus files (#5179).
- **Note the npm package split**: `web-tree-sitter` is at **0.27.0** (2026-08-30), but the `tree-sitter` **node binding** npm package is still at **0.25.1**. They do not track. Also, `v0.26.13` (2026-08-23) is a live maintenance line with parser fixes (`MISSING` node queries, case-insensitive pattern folding, wide error nodes) — you can take those without moving to 0.27. https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter/releases/tag/v0.26.13

---

# WHAT WE SHOULD ACT ON

1. **Do not ship on `@keyhive/keyhive@0.1.0-alpha.8` as a security boundary.** PR #216 (2026-08-27) fixes transitive authority exceeding the weakest link on the best path, and **Relay access to a group transitively granting CGKA-tree membership in a document** — with tests that fail on the pre-fix code. It landed *after* alpha.8 and is unreleased. This lands squarely on our `#has`/Relay cap-tier model and the face-join plane law. Track for alpha.9 or build from `main`.
2. **Bump `@noble/curves` to ≥2.3.0 now** — the X25519 remote timing leak (4.036 bits of long-term key) is the only real vulnerability in this whole sweep, and our persona/ECDH paths use exactly that primitive. Take 2.4.0 for the identity-point rejection in ECDH/ECDSA while you are there. Re-baseline any perf assumption via `Point.BASE.precompute(8)`.
3. **Move the mesh to `2.6.0-subduction.48`** for the compaction data-loss fix (blobs deleted before they could be decrypted). Pair it with ARK `0.5.0-alpha.7`. Treat the `authors` tag as parked, and treat ARK's TypeScript sync-protocol files as condemned — the WASM-over-Rust refactor is announced.
4. **Read `onomancy` before more naming work.** It is I&S solving our exact `lar:///` problem — self-certifying Automerge doc root, local petnames, DNSSEC-rooted global names from the IANA root KSK, with `onomancy_keyhive` doing delegation-chain replay behind an `AuthorityVerifier`. Our DNS-01/split-horizon path and the attestation-seat ruling should be collided against it.
5. **Regenerate the tree-sitter grammar `.wasm` with the 0.27 CLI** before adopting web-tree-sitter 0.27 — the ABI is stable at 15 but the dynamic-linking format is not. Or hold at the `0.26.13` maintenance line, which carries the parser fixes without the move.

## Wire-incompatible flag-days across a mesh

- **Keyhive #213 (2026-08-15) — BeeKEM independently-sampled secrets replacing ratcheting.** Confirmed as the flag day you named. Old and new nodes derive path secrets differently; mixed-version peers cannot agree on group keys. `0.1.0-alpha.8` is the first release carrying it.
- **Keyhive #216** changes *authorization verdicts*, not the wire — but a patched node and an unpatched node will disagree about who is in a CGKA tree, which is a split-brain of a different and nastier kind.
- **Subduction's 1-byte per-type version field: decoders reject unsupported versions outright.** Any bump to a `ST`/`SU` type version is a hard flag day with no graceful-parse path. There is no negotiation in the handshake — worth raising with I&S, and worth noting that Jazz just added exactly that (protocol-version check in the handshake with an explicit update prompt).
- **`bijoux` width variants are explicitly not wire-compatible** with each other and carry no self-describing tag. Subduction switched to it (#277, 2026-07-29). If we encode with a different width than a peer decodes, it silently mis-parses.
- **Iroh 1.1.0's `CustomAddr` serialization fix** (#4465) is marked breaking — relevant if we take the Iroh/QUIC transport.
- **OpenMLS 0.9.0 dropped non-self-describing storage formats** (opt-in `migration-import`) — a storage flag day, not a wire one, but the same class.
- **Not a flag day:** tree-sitter 0.27 (ABI 15 unchanged), and none of the four noble 2.4.0 releases.
